@@ -967,6 +967,27 @@ to all the metrics scraped by the given `vmagent` instance:
 
 See also [how to shard data among multiple remote storage systems](#sharding-among-remote-storages).
 
+## Limiting the number of targets per job
+
+Auto-discovery sources such as cloud APIs, orchestrators, or service catalogs may occasionally
+misbehave (for example, because of a broken tag filter or an overly broad catalog query) and return
+a much bigger number of targets than expected for a given `job_name`. Since `vmagent` scrapes all
+discovered targets by default, such an incident can spike CPU and memory usage and degrade scraping
+for every other job running on the same `vmagent` instance.
+
+The `max_scrape_targets_per_job` [scrape_config](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/#scrape_configs)
+option (and the corresponding `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` command-line flag, which sets the default
+for jobs without an explicit `max_scrape_targets_per_job` value) limits the number of active scrape targets
+a single `job_name` can have at any point in time. This limit is enforced regardless of which service discovery
+mechanism (`static_configs`, `kubernetes_sd_configs`, `ec2_sd_configs`, etc.) produced the targets for the job.
+
+If the number of discovered targets for a job exceeds the configured limit, then only up to the limit are scraped,
+and the rest are excluded. `vmagent` keeps running normally instead of crashing or dropping the whole job. The
+number of excluded targets for the affected job is shown at the `/targets` and `/service-discovery` pages, and it
+is also exposed via the `vm_promscrape_scrape_pool_targets_truncated{scrape_job="<job_name>"}` metric.
+
+By default there is no limit, so this feature doesn't affect existing setups unless it is explicitly configured.
+
 ## High availability
 
 It is possible to run multiple **identically configured** `vmagent` instances or `vmagent`
